      "@type": "Dataset",
      "name": "Data Center Physical Relocation Operations",
      "description": "Data center physical relocation operations: the physical layer of a data center move — servers, populated and de-racked racks and cabinets, storage and network equipment moved between server rooms, colocation facilities and data centers in Northern Virginia and the I-81 corridor — run as a planned, labelled, documented sequence. The published sequence runs: survey and floor-load analysis — the plan, not the truck, is agreed first in the Detailed Project form, the flat rate is quoted in writing before move day, and populated racks and cabinets in the 1,500–3,500 lb band are surveyed under SIR Structural Integrity Relocation and the KIM-GS Protocol with an L/360 floor-deflection check at origin and destination; inventory and chain-of-custody manifest — every item is inventoried and labelled before anything is unplugged, and the manifest records each asset's tag, its serial number as provided by the client, its rack and U position, and photograph points; disconnect and de-install — working to the sequence fixed at survey with the client's IT team, the crew shuts down and unplugs device by device and performs the physical de-rack work, and elements requiring certified specialists are handled by the certified contractors the company engages; pack and seal — equipment travels padded, wrapped and strapped, with anti-static-aware packing for servers and sensitive electronics (anti-static bubble wrap and ESD shielding bags for loose components, foam-lined server shipping crates for racked units, cable labels and tags on every run), custom crating for the pieces that need it, and tamper-evident seals whose numbers are logged on the manifest; the packing materials a job consumes are purchased for each project and scoped inside the flat rate; chain-of-custody transport, strapped and padded in the planned order, with seal numbers verified at departure and arrival; placement at the destination in the labelled order, rack and U position per the manifest, reconnected and re-cabled to the manifest and powered up to the scope agreed at survey, verified with the client's team before the crew leaves; and haul-out — equipment not going to the destination, and retired equipment at refresh or exit, packed, sealed and manifested the same way and transported to the destination the client designates, including the certified ITAD partner engaged for the job. Not published and not claimed for this service: workload migration, application discovery or dependency mapping, recommissioning, backup verification or rollback — the client's IT team or integrator owns the logical move; and data sanitization or destruction to any standard, which belongs to the certified ITAD partner engaged for the job alone. The company claims no IT certifications of its own; elements of a job that require certified specialists are handled by the certified contractors the company engages.",
